For help, type "help".
(debug) help/h           Print help for all commands or for one command
run/r            Run the program from the beginning
start            Run the program and stop at the first line of main()
continue/c       Continue until a breakpoint or the end of the program
step/s           Step to the next source line, entering calls
stepi/si         Step one instruction, entering calls
next/n           Step to the next source line, stepping over calls
nexti/ni         Step one instruction, stepping over calls
finish/fin       Run until the selected function returns
info/i           Show breakpoints, locals, files, ...; see "help info"
break/b          Set a breakpoint; see "help break" for locations
tbreak/tb        Set a breakpoint that is deleted when hit
delete/d         Delete breakpoints, or all breakpoints
disable          Disable breakpoints
enable           Enable breakpoints
backtrace/bt     Print the call stack
frame/f          Select a stack frame, or describe the selected frame
up               Select the frame of the caller
down             Select the frame of the callee
print/p          Print a variable; see "help print"
list/l           List source lines around a location or the current line
source           Print source with execution results for the last <count> lines
display          Print information while stepping; see "help display"
undisplay        Stop printing information while stepping
quit/q           Quit the debugger
Press <enter> to repeat step, next, continue, or list. Type "help <command>" for more.
(debug) break [location]  (alias: b)
  Set a breakpoint; see "help break" for locations
A location is one of:
  <line>             a line in the current file
  <file>:<line>      a line in a file; <file> may be a path, a path suffix,
  <file> <line>        or a file name with or without .v3
  <function>         the first line of a function, e.g. main, Point.sum, Point.new
A breakpoint on a line without code moves to the next line with code.
Without a location, "break" sets a breakpoint at the current line.
(debug) break [location]  (alias: b)
  Set a breakpoint; see "help break" for locations
A location is one of:
  <line>             a line in the current file
  <file>:<line>      a line in a file; <file> may be a path, a path suffix,
  <file> <line>        or a file name with or without .v3
  <function>         the first line of a function, e.g. main, Point.sum, Point.new
A breakpoint on a line without code moves to the next line with code.
Without a location, "break" sets a breakpoint at the current line.
(debug) print <expr>  (alias: p)
  Print a variable; see "help print"
An expression is a local variable, parameter, field of "this", component, or
global variable, followed by any number of selectors:
  .field  .0  .length  [index]
For example: p.x, args[0].length, t.1, Util.count
(debug) breakpoints/b    Print information about breakpoints
locals/l         Print the local variables of the selected frame
inputs/i         Print information about inputs of next instruction
variable/v       Print detail information about a variable <index>(es) separated by space
files/f          List the files in the program
(debug) trace/t          Trace execution for each instruction
source/s         Print source after each stepping instruction
callee-source/ss Print callee's source as well
